W("Reference to context: 'It was an ordinary window, against which now the night was pressing blackly.' (i) Whose eyes went to the window? (ii) What does the description hint? (iii) Which later event shows its importance?", 3, "(i) Fowler's. (1) (ii) The window is the only exit and the darkness hides what lies outside; it is a hint that it holds the trick of the story. (1) (iii) Max escapes through it, believing there is a balcony, and falls. (1)", "M"),
W("How does Ausable make Max believe in the balcony? Write four points.", 3, "He complains that someone has got in 'twice in a month' through the balcony; he explains that the room used to be part of a large unit; he says the next room used to be the living room with the balcony; and that it can be reached from an empty room two doors down, which the management promised to block. (3)", "M"),
W("Why does Ausable say that 'thirty-one minutes' remain? What does this show about him?", 3, "He gives the exact time because the appointment is for twelve-thirty and he wants to seem tired and calm. It shows that he is precise and unruffled, and also that he is playing for time while waiting for Henry. (3)", "M"),
W("Describe how the story reaches its climax in four or five sentences.", 3, "A knock at the door frightens Max. Ausable says it is the police, who will shoot if he does not answer. Max angrily backs to the window and swings his legs over the sill. As the doorknob turns, he drops towards the balcony and screams. The door opens and only a waiter with a tray stands there. (3)", "M"),
W("Why was Fowler white-faced after the waiter left?", 3, "He had believed that police were at the door and that Max was on the balcony. The waiter's arrival and Ausable's words 'There were no police' and 'there is no balcony' showed him that the whole matter had been a trick and that Max had fallen to his death from the sixth floor. (3)", "M"),
W("Vocabulary in context: 'You are disillusioned,' Ausable told him. Explain the word and the cause.", 2, "'Disillusioned' means disappointed on finding that something is not as good or exciting as one thought. Fowler is disappointed because the spy is dull and ordinary, not romantic. (2)", "M"),
